WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.733
Singular value decomposition.

2
00:00:03.033 --> 00:00:07.728
One factorization works on any matrix, and it explains why LoRA works.

3
00:00:07.728 --> 00:00:08.667
Let's see how.

4
00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:13.200
Any matrix, square or not, factors into three pieces.

5
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.700
Reading right to left, V transpose is a rotation.

6
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.745
Sigma is a diagonal matrix that stretches along each axis.

7
00:00:20.745 --> 00:00:25.200
Its entries are the singular values, sorted from biggest to smallest.

8
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.367
And U is a second rotation.

9
00:00:28.567 --> 00:00:31.967
So any transformation is just a three step pipeline.

10
00:00:32.267 --> 00:00:33.467
A vector comes in.

11
00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:36.967
It is rotated into the matrix's natural axes.

12
00:00:37.267 --> 00:00:39.000
Stretched by the singular values.

13
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.633
And rotated again into the output space.

14
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:44.400
Here is the killer property.

15
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:48.051
In real data the singular values fall off quickly.

16
00:00:48.051 --> 00:00:54.083
Keep only the top k and drop the rest, and you get the best possible rank k approximation.

17
00:00:54.083 --> 00:00:57.300
That is why most matrices are secretly low rank.

18
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.433
In NumPy it takes a few lines.

19
00:01:00.733 --> 00:01:05.600
We factor a matrix of two hundred timesteps by sixty four telemetry channels.

20
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.533
Then keep only the five strongest directions.

21
00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:10.655
And measure how much we lost.

22
00:01:10.655 --> 00:01:13.167
For real telemetry, that error is small.

23
00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:16.500
This is the intuition behind LoRA.

24
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:22.867
Fine tuning a model means a weight change of four thousand ninety six by four thousand ninety six.

25
00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:28.067
LoRA bets that it is roughly rank sixteen, so it trains two skinny matrices instead.

26
00:01:28.367 --> 00:01:31.074
That is under one percent of the parameters.

27
00:01:31.074 --> 00:01:34.767
It works because singular values of real updates decay fast.

28
00:01:35.967 --> 00:01:36.772
Let's recap.

29
00:01:36.772 --> 00:01:40.667
Any matrix is a rotation, a stretch, and another rotation.

30
00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:43.933
Sigma holds the singular values, largest first.

31
00:01:44.233 --> 00:01:47.700
Keeping the top k gives the best low rank approximation.

32
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.343
And that is the idea behind LoRA.

33
00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:51.833
Next up: derivatives.
